00:00:00.172 --> 00:00:01.880
JENNY BROWN: Last
week, it was papercuts,

00:00:01.880 --> 00:00:05.030
and this week, it's elephants
or something like that.

00:00:05.030 --> 00:00:06.800
Because today, on
Cloud Storage bytes,

00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:08.870
we're talking about
large file uploads

00:00:08.870 --> 00:00:11.360
and how you can move
mountains in mere moments.

00:00:11.360 --> 00:00:13.860
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:15.860 --> 00:00:18.410
Sometimes, you've got
very large files that just

00:00:18.410 --> 00:00:20.428
seem to crawl during upload.

00:00:20.428 --> 00:00:22.970
Let's say you've got a data set
with files from a few hundred

00:00:22.970 --> 00:00:26.210
megabytes up to two
gigs, and this data set

00:00:26.210 --> 00:00:28.040
is really giving you trouble.

00:00:28.040 --> 00:00:29.780
If you take a peek
under the hood,

00:00:29.780 --> 00:00:31.460
you'd notice a
slight upper limit

00:00:31.460 --> 00:00:33.710
in terms of upload speeds.

00:00:33.710 --> 00:00:36.680
Let's also assume you're
using the Python API,

00:00:36.680 --> 00:00:40.520
meaning our trusty gsutil now
warns you that the file size is

00:00:40.520 --> 00:00:41.940
too large.

00:00:41.940 --> 00:00:44.000
If you take a closer
look at that warning,

00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:47.330
it's making a potentially
game-changing suggestion--

00:00:47.330 --> 00:00:50.570
use parallel composite uploads
for the best performance

00:00:50.570 --> 00:00:53.660
when dealing with one
or more large files.

00:00:53.660 --> 00:00:55.700
During parallel
composite uploads,

00:00:55.700 --> 00:00:59.280
gsutil splits your larger
files into smaller pieces,

00:00:59.280 --> 00:01:00.980
and then uploads
them in parallel,

00:01:00.980 --> 00:01:04.220
and then composes them back
together in your GCS bucket.

00:01:04.220 --> 00:01:06.650
It even cleans up
after itself, removing

00:01:06.650 --> 00:01:08.870
all temporary components.

00:01:08.870 --> 00:01:11.120
Just like what we saw
in our parallel uploads

00:01:11.120 --> 00:01:13.520
for the smaller files,
composite uploads

00:01:13.520 --> 00:01:16.940
dramatically improve the
performance with larger files.

00:01:16.940 --> 00:01:20.090
On some tests, there's as
much as a two-time improvement

00:01:20.090 --> 00:01:22.790
in the upload speeds, which
means you're well on your way

00:01:22.790 --> 00:01:24.500
to moving that mountain.

00:01:24.500 --> 00:01:26.720
There's more to come about
large file performance,

00:01:26.720 --> 00:01:29.160
but we're going to save
that for another episode.

00:01:29.160 --> 00:01:31.820
If you want to know more about
parallel composite uploads

00:01:31.820 --> 00:01:34.980
for large files, check out
the links in the description.

00:01:34.980 --> 00:01:38.030
And as always, don't forget
to subscribe, give us a like,

00:01:38.030 --> 00:01:40.310
and tell us what you want
to learn about Google Cloud

00:01:40.310 --> 00:01:41.240
Storage.

00:01:41.240 --> 00:01:43.640
Thank you for joining us
for this quick byte of cloud

00:01:43.640 --> 00:01:44.270
storage.

00:01:44.270 --> 00:01:45.440
See you next time.

